  intro="This is the chart where honesty matters most: diet has a limited role in pigmentation. Here is what it genuinely contributes, and what it does not."
>
  <div class="note note-warn">
    <h3>Read this part first</h3>
    <p>
      No food lightens pigmentation. The evidence for diet in melasma and other pigmentation is
      weaker than for acne or hair fall, and the industry selling glutathione tablets and
      "skin-whitening" supplements is built on that gap.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Sunscreen, used daily, outperforms every dietary change on this page combined.</strong>
      What follows is worth doing — it supports skin repair and reduces oxidative damage — but it
      is a supporting measure, not a treatment.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Antioxidants — the part with some basis</h2>

  <p>
    Oxidative stress from UV contributes to pigment production. Dietary antioxidants will not
    remove existing pigmentation, but they support the skin's own defences alongside sun
    protection.
  </p>

  <h3>Vitamin C</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Amla — among the richest sources available, and cheap</li>
    <li>Guava, orange, sweet lime, lemon</li>
    <li>Capsicum, tomato, cabbage</li>
    <li>Coriander and mint, used generously</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Vitamin E</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Almonds, sunflower seeds, groundnuts</li>
    <li>Wheat germ, cold-pressed oils</li>
    <li>Green leafy vegetables</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Carotenoids and polyphenols</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Carrots, pumpkin, sweet potato, papaya</li>
    <li>Tomatoes, especially cooked — lycopene is better absorbed that way</li>
    <li>Green tea</li>
    <li>Turmeric in cooking, with black pepper and a fat to help absorption</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Nutrients where a deficiency genuinely matters</h2>

  <table>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row" style="width:30%">Vitamin B12</th>
        <td>Deficiency causes its own pigmentation, often on the knuckles, palms and soles. Common in long-term vegetarians. Worth testing rather than guessing. Found in dairy, eggs, fish; vegetarians may need a supplement.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Folate</th>
        <td>Green leafy vegetables, dals, citrus. Deficiency contributes to the same pattern as B12.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Vitamin D</th>
        <td>Widely deficient in India. Relevant to overall skin health. Test rather than assume — food alone rarely corrects a real deficiency.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Iron</th>
        <td>Severe deficiency can affect skin tone and dullness. Covered in more detail in the hair fall chart.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>A day, put together</h2>

  <table>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row" style="width:26%">Early morning</th>
        <td>Amla juice or a whole amla in season · or warm water with lemon</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Breakfast</th>
        <td>Poha or upma with vegetables · or eggs with roti · plus a citrus fruit</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Mid-morning</th>
        <td>Green tea, or a guava</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Lunch</th>
        <td>Roti · dal · a carotenoid-rich sabzi (carrot, pumpkin, palak) · curd · tomato and cucumber salad with lemon</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Evening</th>
        <td>A handful of almonds and walnuts</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Dinner</th>
        <td>Roti or rice · paneer, dal or fish · cooked tomato-based vegetables</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What to be sceptical about</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Glutathione tablets and injections for skin lightening.</strong> Heavily marketed in
      India. Oral glutathione is poorly absorbed, and the evidence for meaningful skin lightening
      is weak. Intravenous glutathione for cosmetic lightening is not an approved use and carries
      real risk.
    </li>
    <li><strong>"Skin whitening" supplements.</strong> Unregulated, often containing undisclosed ingredients</li>
    <li><strong>Detox and alkaline water routines.</strong> No effect on pigmentation</li>
    <li><strong>Lemon or other acids applied directly to skin.</strong> Causes irritation, and irritation causes more pigment in Indian skin — the opposite of what is intended</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>The two things that actually move the needle</h3>
    <p>
      Daily broad-spectrum sunscreen — tinted, with iron oxide, if melasma is the problem — and
      the right topical treatment, used consistently. Diet supports both. It does not substitute
      for either.
    </p>
  </div>
</Diet>
